## 6. Step 5: iOS Scheme Configuration in Xcode

When building iOS binaries on Bitrise macOS virtual machines, the cloud runner does not have a graphical interface. It relies on **Shared Schemes** in Xcode to know which targets to compile.

### 6.1 Step 1: Mark Schemes as Shared

By default, Xcode marks new schemes as *private* (stored in <code>xcuserdata/</code>, which is excluded by <code>.gitignore</code>). If you do not mark your scheme as **Shared**, Bitrise will report: <code>Scheme not found in workspace</code>.

1. Open your project in Xcode: <code>open ios/MyMobileApp.xcworkspace</code>.
2. In the top toolbar, click the Scheme dropdown and select **Manage Schemes...**
3. Locate your application scheme (e.g., <code>MyMobileApp</code>).
4. Check the **Shared** checkbox in the rightmost column.
5. Click **Close**.

<div class="portal-screenshot-box">
  <div class="portal-screenshot-header">
    <span class="portal-screenshot-badge">SCREENSHOT</span>
    <span class="portal-screenshot-title">Xcode Manage Schemes - Shared Checkbox</span>
  </div>
  <p class="portal-screenshot-desc">
    <strong>Where to capture:</strong> Open Xcode &gt; Menu: <span class="portal-screenshot-path">Product &gt; Scheme &gt; Manage Schemes...</span> Highlight the active scheme row with the <strong>Shared</strong> checkbox checked on the far right.
  </p>
</div>

### 6.2 Step 2: Commit the Shared Scheme to Git

Once marked as shared, Xcode creates a descriptor at:
<code>ios/MyMobileApp.xcodeproj/xcshareddata/xcschemes/MyMobileApp.xcscheme</code>

Stage and commit this file to Git:

```bash
git add ios/MyMobileApp.xcodeproj/xcshareddata/xcschemes/MyMobileApp.xcscheme
git commit -m "chore(ios): mark Xcode scheme as shared for CI execution"
```

