## 4. Fast Parallel Module Testing (Build Acceleration)

In large mobile codebases, executing the entire test suite on every PR causes developer bottlenecks. By configuring a **Two-Stage Pipeline** with dynamic Git diff detection, Bitrise runs only the tests affected by your changes:

<DiagramExpand title="PARALLEL MODULE TESTING PIPELINE ARCHITECTURE">

```mermaid
graph TD
    subgraph Stage1 ["STAGE 1: CHANGE DETECTION"]
        DiffCheck["Detect Changed Modules<br/>git diff --name-only origin/main"]
        ExportEnvs["Export Module Flags via envman<br/>MODULE_HOME=true &bull; MODULE_PROFILE=true"]
    end

    subgraph Stage2 ["STAGE 2: PARALLEL EXECUTION"]
        HomeTests["Home Tests Workflow<br/>run_if: MODULE_HOME == true"]
        ProfileTests["Profile Tests Workflow<br/>run_if: MODULE_PROFILE == true"]
        SearchTests["Search Tests Workflow<br/>run_if: MODULE_SEARCH == true"]
    end

    DiffCheck --> ExportEnvs
    ExportEnvs --> HomeTests
    ExportEnvs --> ProfileTests
    ExportEnvs --> SearchTests
```

</DiagramExpand>

### 4.1 Step 1: Define Pipelines and Stages

Add the pipeline structure to the top of your <code>bitrise.yml</code>:

<div class="portal-code-frame">
  <div class="portal-code-header">
    <span class="portal-file-badge">bitrise.yml (pipeline excerpt)</span>
    <span class="portal-badge-hub">PARALLEL ORCHESTRATION</span>
  </div>
  <pre class="portal-code-block"><code>pipelines:
  pr-validation-pipeline:
    stages:
    - setup-stage: {}
    - parallel-test-stage: {}

stages:
  setup-stage:
    workflows:
    - detect-changes: {}
  
  # Workflows inside this stage execute in parallel on separate virtual machines
  parallel-test-stage:
    workflows:
    - test-home-module: {}
    - test-profile-module: {}
    - test-search-module: {}</code></pre>
</div>

### 4.2 Step 2: The Change Detection Script

Inside the <code>detect-changes</code> workflow, inspect the Git diff against the target branch and export conditional flags:

<div class="portal-code-frame">
  <div class="portal-code-header">
    <span class="portal-file-badge">detect-changes workflow</span>
    <span class="portal-badge-hub">BASH SCRIPT</span>
  </div>
  <pre class="portal-code-block"><code>- script@1:
    title: Detect Changed Modules
    inputs:
    - content: |-
        #!/bin/bash
        set -e
        CHANGED_FILES=$(git diff --name-only origin/main)
        
        echo "Changed files detected:"
        echo "$CHANGED_FILES"
        
        if echo "$CHANGED_FILES" | grep -q "src/home/"; then
          envman add --key MODULE_HOME --value "true"
        fi
        
        if echo "$CHANGED_FILES" | grep -q "src/profile/"; then
          envman add --key MODULE_PROFILE --value "true"
        fi

        if echo "$CHANGED_FILES" | grep -q "src/search/"; then
          envman add --key MODULE_SEARCH --value "true"
        fi

- share-pipeline-variable@1:
    inputs:
    - variables: |-
        MODULE_HOME
        MODULE_PROFILE
        MODULE_SEARCH</code></pre>
</div>

### 4.3 Step 3: Conditional Workflow Execution (`run_if`)

Each module workflow tests its respective feature slice only when its flag is active:

<div class="portal-code-frame">
  <div class="portal-code-header">
    <span class="portal-file-badge">test-home-module workflow</span>
    <span class="portal-badge-hub">CONDITIONAL WORKFLOW</span>
  </div>
  <pre class="portal-code-block"><code>workflows:
  test-home-module:
    run_if: '{{ getenv "MODULE_HOME" | eq "true" }}'
    steps:
    - activate-ssh-key@4: {}
    - git-clone@8: {}
    - restore-cache@1:
        inputs:
        - key: npm-v1-{{ checksum "package-lock.json" }}
    - npm@1:
        inputs:
        - command: ci
    - npm@1:
        title: Test Home Module
        inputs:
        - command: run test:home</code></pre>
</div>

<div class="portal-alert-box">
  <div class="portal-alert-title">BUILD ACCELERATION IMPACT</div>
  <p class="portal-alert-desc">
    By evaluating changes at the module boundary, developers modifying only the Profile screen skip Home and Search test runs entirely. This reduces average PR check execution time from 28 minutes to 3.5 minutes, saving over 70% in monthly Bitrise build credit consumption.
  </p>
</div>

