### 3.2 How the `sign-apk` Step Operates at Runtime

When your workflow executes the <code>sign-apk@1</code> step, Bitrise performs the following sequence:

1. Downloads the encrypted Keystore file into a temporary, isolated directory on the runner:
   <code>/tmp/bitrise_signing_xxxx/release.keystore</code>
2. Exports the temporary file path as <code>$BITRISEIO_ANDROID_KEYSTORE_URL</code>.
3. Injects the credentials into environment variables:
   * <code>$BITRISEIO_ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD</code>
   * <code>$BITRISEIO_ANDROID_KEYSTORE_ALIAS</code>
   * <code>$BITRISEIO_ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD</code>
4. Signs the compiled binary using <code>apksigner</code> / <code>jarsigner</code> with v1, v2, and v3 signature schemes.
5. Emits the signed binary to <code>$BITRISE_SIGNED_APK_PATH</code> or <code>$BITRISE_SIGNED_AAB_PATH</code>.
6. Automatically shreds the temporary keystore file when the build terminates.

---

## 4. Step 3: Apple App Store Connect API Key (`.p8`)

Historically, iOS code signing relied on developer Apple IDs combined with SMS 2-Factor Authentication. In enterprise automation, this caused frequent build failures when 2FA sessions expired every 30 days.

Modern Bitrise pipelines use **App Store Connect API Keys (`.p8`)**, which provide permanent, headless authentication directly through Apple APIs.

### 4.1 Generating the API Key in App Store Connect

1. Log into **App Store Connect**: <code>https://appstoreconnect.apple.com</code>.
2. In the top navigation, click **Users and Access**.
3. Select the **Integrations** tab &gt; **App Store Connect API**.
4. Click the **+** (Generate API Key) button.
5. In the modal:
   * **Name**: <code>Bitrise-CI-Delivery-Key</code>
   * **Access**: Select **App Manager** (or Admin).
6. Click **Generate**.
7. Note the **Issuer ID** at the top of the page (UUID string).
8. Note the **Key ID** on the generated row (10 alphanumeric characters).
9. Click **Download API Key** to retrieve the <code>AuthKey_<KeyID>.p8</code> file.

<div class="portal-alert-box">
  <div class="portal-alert-title">ONE-TIME DOWNLOAD WARNING</div>
  <p class="portal-alert-desc">
    Apple only allows you to download the <code>.p8</code> file once. Store the downloaded file immediately in your enterprise secure password vault. If lost, the key cannot be recovered; you must revoke it and generate a new key pair.
  </p>
</div>

---

### 4.2 Uploading the Apple API Key to Bitrise

1. Open your app in Bitrise &gt; **Workflow Editor &gt; Code Signing Tab**.
2. Scroll to the **Apple Service Account API Keys** section.
3. Click **Add API Key**.
4. Fill in the parameters:
   * **Name**: <code>Enterprise-AppStore-API-Key</code>
   * **Key ID**: Enter the 10-character key ID from Apple (e.g., <code>2X9R4ABCD2</code>).
   * **Issuer ID**: Enter the UUID from Apple (e.g., <code>57246542-96fe-1a63-e053-0824d011072a</code>).
   * **Private Key**: Upload the <code>AuthKey_2X9R4ABCD2.p8</code> file.
5. Click **Save**.

